<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key><string>English</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key><string>AMDMapperProbe</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>org.example.AMDMapperProbe</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key><string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key><string>AMDMapperProbe</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key><string>KEXT</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key><string>0.97.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key><string>0.97.0</string>
    <!-- Client requests must fit [OSBundleCompatibleVersion, CFBundleVersion].
         Existing mapper ABI1 clients remain compatible with 0.53.0;
         Round54 clients require 0.61.0 for a pre-open claim on their selected PCI requester. -->
    <key>OSBundleCompatibleVersion</key><string>0.53.0</string>
    <!--
      0.71.0 Round 65: both device domains complete PM prepare and restore invalidation, with the shared Controller coordinating a single suspend and restart.\n      0.72.0 Round 68: coordinated dual-domain System PM deferred; the verified ordinary dual-NIC lifecycle is restored.
      0.75.0 Round 69: with amdiommu_autoattach=1 the selected device receives iommu-parent at first publish, for Apple drivers that cannot call the private claim; new mapping-capacity and rejection-reason properties.
      0.73.0 Round 66: dual-domain PM only allows an explicit arm after boot, so a PM-capable boot cannot affect the ordinary NIC lifecycle.
      Version history:
        0.70.0 Round 63: the device's DTE read/write authorization is revoked on deactivation; recovery must first complete device and domain invalidation.
        0.69.0 Round 62: the shared command ring reuses the last read-back HEAD, refreshing hardware registers only when reserved space is insufficient.
        0.68.0 Round 61: ordinary page-range exact-segment invalidation, each batch sharing one completion wait; reclaimed only after confirmation.
        0.67.0 Round 60: ordinary multi-page DMA prefers naturally aligned IOVAs, keeping the original allocation path under fragmentation; statistics invalidation commands cover pages.
        0.66.0 Round 59: a single ordinary DMA mapping supports 128 pages and 128 physical segments, exporting all pages per ABI3.
        0.65.0 Round 58: physical addresses given by descriptors are saved per in-domain IOVA page; active and historical snapshots keep the original ABI2 format.
        0.64.0 Round 57: mapping record slots are managed by a per-domain occupancy bitmap; keeps lowest-free-slot selection, the start page index and synchronous reclamation.
        0.63.0 Round 56: ordinary unmap locates the record by this domain's starting IOVA page, keeping full ownership checks and reclamation after synchronous invalidation.
        0.62.0 Round 55: the IOVA bitmap searches 64-bit words for the lowest aligned free span; keeps the R54 driver ABI and synchronous reclamation.
        0.61.0 Round 54: explicit requesters claim a device domain from the client before PCI open; ordinary DMA and per-device invalidation paths follow R53.
        0.60.0 Round 53: the device domain is selected by explicit requester for the RTL8111/RTL8127 mix; unselected devices stay pass-through, sharing the original ordinary-DMA backend.
        0.59.0 Round 52: two RTL8127s on one controller; per-device IOMapper, domain, page table and IOVA pool, with shared command ring synchronization.
        0.58.0 Round 51: ordinary map invalidation is selected by PCI NPCache; without an NP cache a new map skips INV/CW, and unmap's synchronous reclamation is unchanged.
        0.57.0 Round 50: ordinary map/unmap invalidate synchronously by the actual IOVA page range; first activation and PM remain whole-domain; PM on-machine verification still TODO.
        0.56.0 Round 49: ordinary map supports 64 pages/64 runs; RX changed to 16 groups of 64 pages each; PM on-machine verification still TODO.
        0.55.0 Round 48: the ordinary mapper uses a low-4GiB IOVA pool, supporting 32-bit DMA address constraints; PM on-machine verification still TODO.
        0.54.0 Round 47: the ordinary auto path gains runtime IVRS/PCI discovery; IOMMU suspend/resume handling added; on-machine PM verification TODO.
        0.53.0 M2-3y Round 44: automatic discovery of ordinary-device IOMappers; a resident controller with generic MD/IODMACommand mapping; the device domain is kept across normal NIC close and open.
        0.52.0 M2-3x Round 43: NIC stop moved earlier, before the DTE switch; the native device domain uses only IOVAs throughout, and exit no longer builds identity recovery mappings.
        0.51.0 M2-3w Round 42: on NIC stop the full ring's initial RX IOVAs are published directly; bootstrap identity is withdrawn before starting TX/RX.
        0.50.0 M2-3v Round 41: fixed the fixed-DMA entry's interrupt re-arming, and recognizes a rebuilt TX ring by its registered object.
        0.49.0 M2-3u Round 40: TX/RX descriptor rings and the statistics buffer use standard DMA-mapped IOVAs; after takeover the active domain keeps no identity leaves.
        0.42.0 M2-3n Round 33: the service object owns the device domain's resources; DMA continues after the init thread returns, and an explicit stop starts a separate teardown thread.
        0.41.0 M2-3m Round 32: dynamic TX IOVA region, independent active mapping records, standard DMA capacity and reuse verification.
        0.40.0 M2-3l Round 31: a dedicated kernel thread maintains the RX1024/TX standard mapping sessions; .session cancels the timed exit,
               after an explicit R31Stop the drain, R29D, retirement confirmation and full recovery proceed as before.
        0.39.0 M2-3k Round 30: RX1024 full-ring standard mapping with cyclic reuse; the 8MiB RX IOVA spans 5 L1 tables,
               unmapped after all physical descriptors are recovered, keeping the R29D tail flow and the standard TX retirement confirmation.
        0.38.0 M2-3i Round 29: after RX release, R29D confirms the tail flow has ended; standard TX confirms retirement by handle,
               and the DTE and command engine are restored only after the software generation and the backend's owner/slot/CW close.
        0.37.0 M2-3h Round 28: 64 RX objects are each mapped once and reused cyclically; draining is published after Disarm,
               TX stays open waiting up to 15 s for the RX drain, with a 120 s bounded authorization covering the 60 s translation window.
        0.36.0 M2-3h Round 27: .std.alltx.rxstd adds a one-shot standard-mapping closed loop for 64 RX spares;
               the token only identifies the run and req is the internal mapping sequence. Four slots and 32 history entries kept.
        0.1.0  M1-A: only verify that copyMapperForDevice(self) can return this mapper.
        0.2.0  M1-B/B1: give a real device's IOMA (pci1022,1481) an iommu-parent and verify.
        0.3.0  B1 fix: fromPath + strict IOName validation.
        0.4.0  B2-0: observe the iovmMapMemory call contract with our own buffer.
        0.5.0  B2-a: implement identity mapping for a physically contiguous single segment and verify fragmentation is rejected.
        0.6.0  B2-b: support a real NIC (RTL8127), mapping table + the amdiommu=arm gate.
        0.7.0  B2-b fix: eliminate the injection race with gIOFirstPublishNotification.
        0.8.0  M2-0b: read-only PCI config space, take the BAR in the IOMMU capability,
               cross-checking against the IVRS base / DSDT _CRS. All read-only, no MMIO touched.
        0.9.0  M2-1: read-only MMIO probing. Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=mmio,
               not executed at all by default; when enabled, reads EFR read-only and compares with the known value,
               stopping immediately on mismatch, writing no registers.
        0.10.0 M2-2a-1: minimal MMIO write test. Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=write.
               Writes a marker value to the DEV_TABLE register once, reads it back, immediately restores the original,
               and confirms CONTROL was not changed. No IOMMU enable, no commands.
        0.11.0 M2-2a-2: build the device table (no enable). Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=table.
               Allocate physically contiguous memory, fill all pass-through DTEs, write the DEV_TABLE base register,
               confirm CONTROL unchanged, restore the original. The IOMMU stays off throughout.
        0.12.0 M2-2a-3: short-window enable test. Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=enable.
               Walks the full Linux early_enable_iommu() sequence: device table + command buffer +
               CMDBUF_EN + IOMMU_EN, reads STATUS then **disables immediately**,
               then restores all registers. An extremely short window, to verify that the enable action itself is safe.
        0.13.0 M2-2b-1: command channel verification. Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=cmd.
               Three memory blocks (device table / command buffer / completion signal cmd_sem),
               issue one CMD_COMPL_WAIT and poll cmd_sem to confirm the hardware really executed it.
               Still a short window: enable -> issue command -> poll -> immediately disable.
        0.24.0 M2-2b-2 Round 9: driver-side records replace ring reading as the primary evidence.
                Round 8's ring-reading scheme had three limitations it could not reconcile: the bytes read
                cannot be proven to belong to one generation (only the frozen tailAfter exists, and a slot
                reused after boundary sampling is indistinguishable); a register naming an address does not
                prove the memory object is still alive; TSO segments' true lengths do not exist in the descriptor.
                Now the driver records metadata at its own submit/complete points and copies a suffix
                into an IORegistry property -- what the probe reads is a copy of an object the driver
                **still holds**, dereferencing no physical address, with pairing done on the driver's own
                cumulative indices (same source as the hardware close pointer).
                Scan and accounting also fixed: the window scan has an explicit cap and records its stop reason
                (an abnormally large delta no longer becomes 2^31 kernel loop iterations); window records use
                dedicated capacity, no longer contending with thousands of historical snapshots; scan quality
                (early stop, lost records, undecodable lengths, overwritten slots) withdraws coverage eligibility
                instead of being merely displayed alongside; "completions > submits" is legal when in-flight items
                existed at window start and is no longer judged an error.
                Time boundaries: the post close-pointer sample moved **inside** the window (round 8 wrapped it,
                a 3.00785 s span over a 3.00018 s window); the translation-activation point
                moved to after both invalidation commands received completions.
        0.23.0 M2-2b-2 Round 8: actually wiring up the production observation flow.
               Round 7's txSlotLifecycle() existed and had regressions, but **production never
               called it**: m2b2CollectTxSegments() accepted tailCum/cloCum and then
               (void)-ed them, labelling every non-zero TX address in-flight as long as a close
               pointer was readable. In round 7's capture tail = clo = 0x6a (queue drained),
               yet every historical address was still reported in-flight.
               Now scanTxWindow() walks by cumulative index over [tailBefore, tailAfter),
               each descriptor carrying its own slot, generation, address, length and completion
               state; a positive close-pointer delta lands on a concrete slot/generation/address/length.
               Also fixed: snapshot hits are no longer reported as "completed ranges"; gaps in the
               snapshot are no longer taken as gaps in the window; coverage is recorded per path (TX
               whole-window coverage does not equal RX whole-window coverage); ring memory gains a
               content fingerprint (the RX last slot's RingEnd, TX address/length self-consistency), so the
               register's self-report is no longer the sole basis; boundary sampling and the ring scan are
               separated, and the scan no longer sits between two close-pointer samples.
        0.22.1 M2-2b-2 Round 7 fix 2: readVerdict must not apply to device-reported addresses.
               0.22.0 applied layoutKnown=false's strict criterion to the ring base as well, so the
               ring could not be read at all: tx-slots=0, raw-offered=0,
               unscanned=0x800 -- the records were right, but the observation was useless. A device-reported
               address (written by the driver, in use by hardware) differs in safety nature
               from an arbitrary value in a descriptor; the former needs only a mapping-ceiling and
               MMIO-hole check.
        0.22.0 M2-2b-2 Round 7 fix: address recording is no longer filtered by the identity-mapping ceiling
               (recording and dereferencing completely separated); insertCoalesced keeps
               updating the envelope even when full (round 6's early break caused txdesc4/5 to fall
               outside their declared envelope-hi); the five counter classes are separate and never
               double-counted; descriptors record address+length in their actual format; lifecycle is
               derived from the driver's cumulative submit/close pointers, no longer uniformly labelled
               live; the close-pointer register is derived from TxConfig rather than elected among
               candidates, and the delta uses MaxTxDescPtrMask=0x7FFFFFFF rather than ring
               modulo 1024 (1000->2024 should be 1024, not 0); the control branch must have an
               actual completion state; page table paths are truly read level by level and the table
               physical address/level/index exported.
        0.21.7 M2-2b-2 Round 6 fix: device DMA observation now takes memory via BAR registers
               (getDeviceMemoryWithRegister) rather than array indices -- the latter
               reads the wrong MMIO region. Register trustworthiness judgement added.
        0.21.0 M2-2b-2 Round 6: device DMA observation (read-only reads of TX/RX rings and descriptors),
               fault attribution classification, pgtsleep verdict priority fix, window structs moved off the stack.
        0.20.0 M2-2b-2: 4-level identity page table + single-device DTE switch. Gated by boot-arg amdiommu=pgt.
               Builds the 4-level table (2 MiB large pages identity-mapping all 16 GiB of RAM), opens the event log,
               enables, CMD_INV_ALL, switches the RTL8127's DTE from pass-through to translation,
               INV_DEV_ENTRY + INV_IOMMU_PAGES, observes the event log for 2 s,
               then switches the DTE back to pass-through and restores all registers.

        0.27.0 M2-3b Round 17: non-identity TX mapping of native mbufs with per-packet reclamation.
                No more staging copies: the packet's own physical segments go straight
                to the mapping backend, keeping each segment's length, order and intra-page offset; descriptors
                use the returned non-identity IOVA. Each request releases its mbuf immediately after hardware
                completion and unmap invalidation, and allows IOVA/page-slot reuse, no longer accumulating until Detach.
                What is bounded is the evidence array (32 generations) and the command budget (160 submissions),
                not the DMA holder; on budget exhaustion it refuses explicitly and leaves no mapping.
                New submit/completion handoff states (kPosting/kSubmitted/kPending),
                so even a very fast completion cannot read or write the same witness across generations.

        0.28.0 M2-3c Round 18: standard IOMapper / IODMACommand wired into real TX.
                The driver builds an IOMemoryDescriptor per packet and constructs an IODMACommand with the
                probe's own IOMapper (not the system mapper); IOKit actually calls
                iovmMapMemory() inside prepare() to produce the non-identity IOVA,
                which the descriptors use; complete() has the framework actually call
                iovmUnmapMemory() to perform unmap and invalidation, after which the descriptor,
                command and mbuf are released. Only single-segment physically contiguous packet layouts are
                supported; everything else is rejected explicitly before prepare(). Also fixes the retire
                timer's active reaping: first acquire __POLLING non-blockingly, skip if held, then
                retire and wait for IOMMU invalidation after release.

      Matches IOResources rather than any PCI device. IOResources is a global resource node holding no
      DMA, so even if this mapper's iovm* calls all fail, no device is harmed by it.
    -->
    <key>IOKitPersonalities</key>
    <dict>
        <key>AMDMapperProbe</key>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>org.example.AMDMapperProbe</string>
            <key>IOClass</key><string>org_example_AMDMapperProbe</string>
            <key>IOMatchCategory</key><string>AMDMapperProbe</string>
            <key>IOProviderClass</key><string>IOResources</string>
            <key>IOResourceMatch</key><string>IOBSD</string>
        </dict>
    </dict>
    <key>OSBundleLibraries</key>
    <dict>
        <key>com.apple.kpi.iokit</key><string>25.0.0</string>
        <key>com.apple.kpi.libkern</key><string>25.0.0</string>
        <key>com.apple.kpi.mach</key><string>25.0.0</string>
        <key>com.apple.kpi.bsd</key><string>25.0.0</string>
        <!--
          0.8.0 addition: a direct dependency on IOPCIFamily.

          Why it must be declared explicitly (a real problem hit at the 0.8.0 deployment):
            M2-0b uses OSDynamicCast(IOPCIDevice, fTarget), and OSDynamicCast
            expands to OSMetaClassBase::safeMetaCast(inst, OSTypeID(IOPCIDevice)),
            where OSTypeID(T) = T::metaClass -- this **references** the
            IOPCIDevice::metaClass symbol.

            kmutil reported:
              Failed to bind '__ZN11IOPCIDevice9metaClassE' ... as could not
              find a kext which exports this symbol

            Yet the symbol does exist in IOPCIFamily's export table (verified). The cause:
            the probe's OSBundleLibraries listed only the kpi.* series and never declared
            its dependency on IOPCIFamily, so at AuxKC link time the linker did not know
            where to find it.

          Version 2.9 matches the measured running version (kmutil showloaded shows
          com.apple.iokit.IOPCIFamily (2.9)).
        -->
        <key>com.apple.iokit.IOPCIFamily</key><string>2.9</string>
        <!-- Runtime IVRS acquisition and IOACPIPlatformDevice metaClass. -->
        <key>com.apple.iokit.IOACPIFamily</key><string>1.4</string>
    </dict>
	<key>OSBundleRequired</key>
	<string>Root</string>
</dict>
</plist>
